# What I Learned from the Simulations

The simulations made the theory much easier to understand because I
could actually watch the communication happen.

## 1. IP address and MAC address have different jobs

I understood that:

-   **IP address** identifies the logical destination.
-   **MAC address** is used for delivery on the local Ethernet network.

A host may know the destination IP address but still need the
destination MAC address before it can send an Ethernet frame on the
local network.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 2. ARP connects IP addressing with MAC addressing

The simulation helped me understand ARP more clearly.

For IPv4:

``` text
Known:
Destination IP

Need:
Destination MAC

Solution:
ARP
```

The sender broadcasts an ARP request, the device with the matching IP
responds with its MAC address, and the sender stores the information in
its ARP table.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 3. Broadcast stays within a broadcast domain

The ARP request was sent as a broadcast.

This demonstrated that switches forward broadcasts within the local
broadcast domain.

Routers are used to separate broadcast domains.

So:

``` text
Switch → forwards local broadcasts
Router → separates broadcast domains
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 4. Routing is needed between different IP networks

If two devices are in the same IP network, they can communicate directly
at the local network level.

If they are in different IP networks, the traffic needs to go through a
router.

Example:

``` text
192.168.1.10
     |
   Switch
     |
   Router
     |
   Switch
     |
192.168.2.10
```

This is why routers are essential for communication between different
networks.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 5. The default gateway is the way out of the local network

The default gateway is the router interface connected to the host's
local network.

If a host wants to reach another network, it sends the traffic to the
default gateway.

``` text
Host
 |
 v
Default Gateway
 |
 v
Other Network
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 6. A router makes decisions using IP addresses

I reinforced the difference between switches and routers:

  Device   Main Layer   Main Address Used
  -------- ------------ -------------------
  Switch   Layer 2      MAC address
  Router   Layer 3      IP address

A switch decides where to forward Ethernet frames based mainly on MAC
addresses.

A router examines the destination IP address and uses its routing table
to determine the next hop.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 7. Packet Tracer Simulation Mode is useful for learning

Simulation Mode allowed me to slow down the communication and inspect
each event.

Instead of only seeing:

``` text
PC → PC
```

I could see what was actually happening:

``` text
ARP Request
     ↓
Broadcast
     ↓
ARP Reply
     ↓
MAC address learned
     ↓
Ethernet frame
     ↓
Packet forwarded
```

This made the concepts of ARP, MAC addresses, IP addresses, broadcasts,
and routing much clearer.

